    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="27">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 27</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="27" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="330" ed="#liebermann"/>And se ðe on gemote mid wiðertihtlan hine sylfne oððon<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-4"/> his mann<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.2-3"/> werige, hæbbe<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.3-2"/> þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> eall<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#missing"/> forspecen, ⁊ ge<hi rend="italic">and</hi>werde<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.5"/> ðam oþrum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.6"/>, swa hundrede<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.7"/> riht þince.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="27" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="330" ed="#liebermann"/>⁊ se on gemote mid wiðertihtlan hine sylfne oððe his man<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-2"/> werie, hæbbe<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.2"/> eall þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> forspecen, ⁊ geandwyrde ðam oðru<hi rend="italic">m</hi>, swa hundrede riht ðynce.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="27" corresp="#wit-a"><pb n="330" ed="#liebermann"/>And se ðe on gemote mid wiðertihtlan hine sylfne oððe his man werige, habbe þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> eal forspecen, ⁊ geandwyrde þam oðran, swa hundred<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-5"/> riht þince.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="27" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="331" ed="#liebermann"/>Et qui in placito se ipsum uel hominem suum distortis compellationibus<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-3"/> defendere presumpserit, habeat totum hoc forspecen (<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.2-2"/>cassatum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.3"/>), et contingat de reliquo, sicut hundreto rectum uidebitur.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="27" corresp="#wit-inst-cn"><pb n="331" ed="#liebermann"/>Si quis in placitis per contrarias calumnias uult defendere se ipsum aut hominem<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-6"/> suum, pro nichilo habeantur illę<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_27.lnote.oe.2-4"/> calumnię, sed respondeat calumnianti se, sicut hundred iudicauerit.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="27" corresp="#wit-cons-cn"><pb n="331" ed="#liebermann"/>Et qui se uel suum hominem in placito defenderit contracalumpniis, totum illud sit perlocutum, et tamen calumpnianti satisfaciat iuxta decretum centenarii.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="27" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="188" ed="#robertson"/>⁊ se on gemote mid wiðertihtlan hine sylfne oððe his man<ptr type="editorNote" n="5" target="#IICn_27.robnote.fn.5"/> werie, hæbbe eall þæt forspecen ⁊ geandwyrde ðam oðrum, swa hundrede riht ðynce.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="268" ed="#rabin-doml"/>And se ðe on gemote mid wiðertihtlan hine sylfne oððon his mann werige, hæbbe þæt eall forspecen, and geandwerde ðam oþrum, swa hundrede riht þince.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="330" ed="#liebermann"/>Und wer im Gericht sich selbst oder seinen Untergebenen vertheidigt durch Widerklage[n?]<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.lnote.de.1"/>, habe dies alles vergeblich gesprochen, und antworte dem Gegner<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_27.lnote.de.2"/>, wie dem Hundertgericht richtig dünkt.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And whoever in court defends himself or his man by counter-accusation(s?), let all of it count as said in vain, and let him answer his opponent as seems right to the hundred.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="189" ed="#robertson"/>And he who in Court tries to protect either himself or one of his men by bringing a countercharge<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IICn_27.robnote.n.1"/> shall have wasted his words, and shall meet the charge brought by his opponent in such a way as the hundred court shall determine.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><pb n="465" ed="#whitelock"/>And he who defends himself or his man in court by a counter-charge shall have spoken this all in vain, and is to answer the other as seems right to the hundred.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="269" ed="#rabin-doml"/>And anyone who seeks to protect himself or his follower in court by bringing a countercharge shall have spoken wholly in vain, and he shall respond to his opponent’s charge in whatever fashion the hundred court thinks best.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">G's ‘ge⁊werde’ (with the Tironian sign for ‘and’).; BR: added from Gesetze I p. 331, October 2026 (MS Cons)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">331</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-iant <hi rend="italic">Hr</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-iant Hr</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-2" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">geänd. im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh.</hi> mann</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">altered in the 16th cent. to mann</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">þ<hi rend="italic">æt im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh. zugefügt</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">þ: æt added in the 16th cent.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-3" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">331</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">suis <hi rend="italic">fügt zu K</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">suis added by K</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">331</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">id est <hi rend="italic">fügt zu T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">id est added by T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">331</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">nur Dm T; fehlt übr.</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">only Dm T; lacking in the others</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-4" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">oþþe <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">oþþe Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.2-3" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">man <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">man Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.3-2" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ealle <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ealle Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ge⁊werde <hi rend="italic">G;</hi> -wyrde <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ge⁊werde G; -wyrde Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ran <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ran Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-ed rihte <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-ed rihte Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-5" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">330</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.1-6" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">331</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">s. h. <hi rend="italic">Di</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">s. h. Di</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.oe.2-4" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">331</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">c. i. <hi rend="italic">Cb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">c. i. Cb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">206</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Die Lateiner verstehen Plural, der zu ihrer Zeit, aber gewöhnlich nicht schon zu Cnuts Zeit, auf -<hi rend="italic">an</hi> endigen kann.—Cons bildet <hi rend="italic">perloqui</hi> mit privativem <hi rend="italic">per-</hi>, wie <hi rend="italic">perfidus, periurus</hi>.—<hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Gegenklage. Mndd. kommt <hi rend="italic">wedderklagen</hi> vor und die Sache im Sachsenspiegel III 12, 1: ‘Kläger braucht dem der <hi rend="italic">weder up ine klaget</hi> nicht zu antworten bis zur Erledigung seiner Klage’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The Latin versions understand a plural, which in their time, though not usually as early as Cnut's, can end in -<hi rend="italic">an</hi>.—Cons forms <hi rend="italic">perloqui</hi> with privative <hi rend="italic">per-</hi>, like <hi rend="italic">perfidus, periurus</hi>.—<hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Gegenklage. Middle Low German has <hi rend="italic">wedderklagen</hi>, and the thing itself occurs in the Sachsenspiegel III 12, 1: ‘the plaintiff need not answer one who <hi rend="italic">weder up ine klaget</hi> until his own suit has been settled’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_27.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">206</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Q nimmt <hi rend="italic">oðran</hi> falsch als Neutrum, ebenso <hi rend="italic">de cetero</hi> Hn 34, 5</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Q wrongly takes <hi rend="italic">oðran</hi> as neuter, likewise <hi rend="italic">de cetero</hi> Hn 34, 5</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_27.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">355</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Cf. Bateson, <hi rend="italic">Borough Customs</hi>, <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi>. p. 9. In Iceland it would seem from the Sagas that such a practice was very common, <hi rend="italic">e.g.</hi> <hi rend="italic">Njalssaga</hi>, Chap. 64.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_27.robnote.fn.5" n="5" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">188</biblScope></bibl><label>man</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Changed in 16th cent. to <hi rend="italic">mann</hi> B.</seg></note>
      </div>
